\details{
Variable columns in \code{"simple"} and \code{"tibble"} output appear in
sorted variable order (\code{sort(vars)}), regardless of the order in which
variables appear in the polynomial's terms.

When the variety has several disconnected components, HMC chains cannot
cross the low-density regions between them, so the share of draws in
each component reflects how many chains initialized near it rather than
the components' relative mass. For such varieties, consider increasing
\code{chains}, or use \code{rejection = TRUE}, which samples components in
proportion to their mass.

With \code{homo = TRUE}, the Stan programs normalize a polynomial system's
residual through its Jacobian, which must have full rank away from the
system's singular points, so systems with dependent equations are
rejected; drop the redundant equations or use \code{homo = FALSE}. With
\code{rejection = TRUE}, draws follow the density \code{\link[=pdvnorm]{pdvnorm()}} defines, and
only systems where it is 0 everywhere off the variety are rejected.

With \code{noise = "domain"}, each draw is \code{x = u + e}, where \code{u} is nearly
uniform on the variety (by arclength, area, and so on, and within the
window \code{w} when one is given) and \code{e} is normal with mean 0 and
covariance from \code{sd} or \code{Sigma}. The points \code{u} are draws of the
homogeneous variety normal with scale \code{pilot_sd}, each moved to the
closest point on the variety; draws whose projection fails, as near
singular points, are replaced by further pilot runs, whose chains
continue the numbering in \code{.chain}. The window bounds \code{u}, not \code{x}.

Uniform points need a variety of finite size: compact, or bounded by
the window \code{w}. On a component where \code{poly} has a factor such as
\code{(x - 1)^2}, the pilot's residual is about half the distance to the
variety, so the pilot gives that component twice its share. For a
single polynomial, \code{rvnorm()} estimates each pilot draw's multiplicity
\code{k} from that ratio and keeps the draw with probability \code{1 / k}. For a
system, whose correction one draw cannot estimate, it stops. The
approximation is of order \code{pilot_sd^2} where the variety is smooth, and
isolated singular points, such as crossings, do not affect it. As with
codomain noise, chains do not cross between disconnected components, so
\code{rejection = TRUE} suits such varieties better. \code{"tibble"} output adds
the points \code{u} as \verb{<var>_proj} columns; \code{"stanfit"} output and
\code{inc_warmup = TRUE} are not available. A given \code{seed} also seeds the
noise, without changing R's random number stream.
}
